  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n***********Today's Severe Potential*************************\nSince the last discussion, SPC has expanded the marginal risk to the \nremainder of the S IN CWA counties, with a slight just clipping \nWashington, Orange, Dubois, and Scott counties. While the upper-\nlevel wind profile and shear is favorable for a wind and low-end \ntornado threat, this risk is entirely based on the potential of \nstorms to form. At the moment, it does not appear that we have had \nor will have enough surface heating for these storms to form. \nHowever, if clearing occurs across Southern Illinois and Indiana \nwithin the next two hours, this threat may become slightly more \nrealistic. This threat should quickly clear out after sunset as we \nlose the surface-based energy to sustain it.\n\n***********Overnight and Friday's Cold Front******************* \nBowling Green has broken their high record for today, although they \nare likely the only ones. Cloud cover has kept the rest of the CWA \nin a fog/drizzle/showery environment today, pushing the high temps \ntoward the low 80s instead. Overnight, our minimum temperatures will \noccur close to sunrise. There should be a northwest to southeast \ngradient, with temperatures in southern IN in the mid-50s and south \ncentral KY in the mid-60s. We expect the cold front to push through \nin the same direction, with it passing over S IN around 08z. This \nmeans that tomorrow's max temps should also occur just after \nsunrise, with highs near 60 for S IN, the Bowling Green Area, and \nthe Louisville area. Highs are likely to be closer to the upper 60s \nfor the Bluegrass and low 70s for Lake Cumberland area. \n\nWith this cold front, expect to see a wind switch from southwesterly \nflow to northwesterly flow. It will be breezy tomorrow, with \nsustained speeds in the 13-15 kt range and gusts up to 25kts. Winds \nwill persist into the overnight hours. A light line of showers is \nalso likely, which should clear out by tomorrow evening. Tomorrow \nnight's min temperatures will be around the 40 degree mark. \n\n**************This weekend's fall weather**********************\n\nSaturday should see some seasonable temperatures, with high \ntemperatures in the 50s. Breezy conditions should persist through \nthe day on Saturday, with wind direction shifting to northeasterly \nthroughout the day as as the surface Low pushes off the coast and a \nsurface high sets up over northern Indiana. Overnight Saturday \nnight, winds should slow down. Frost is certainly not out of the \nquestion early Sunday morning, but winds are the limiting factors. \nTemperatures will be in the low to mid-30s across the region for \novernight lows. We do warm back up a bit on Sunday, with highs in \nthe mid-60s as the High pressure pushes towards Pennysylvania, where \nit stagnates over the mid-Appalachians. This will lead to a \ncontinues wind shift, to the point where we likely have southerly \nflow Sunday evening, albeit light flow. Expect chlly overnight lows \nagain, in the 30s.\n\nThe stagnant high pressure should keep us seasonable for the end of \nnext week, with highs in the 70s and lows in the 40s. Expect mostly \nlight winds throughout. However, clear skies should be visible for \nsome gorgeous fall sunsets.",
